														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2106240">
									

<p>I changed my mind about this article So Many Times. Author, you have made me dizzy.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Specifics</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Here&nbsp;we&nbsp;go</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Firstly, &quot;The Suge Knights&quot; is hilarious. I love that. It's almost enough for an instant upvote.</p>
<p>For the most part the tone is good, though I noticed it wobbled a couple times. And example would be the phrase, &quot;the manner in which SCP-2137-1 instances are copied onto SCP-2137 is still a <strong>complete mystery.</strong>&quot; I would recommend something along the lines of &quot;is currently not understood.&quot; or &quot;has yet to be ascertained.&quot;</p>
<p>Strike outs and cross references are usually enough to earn a downvote. I would advise that you find a way to have your article rely on them far less. SCP-██ conveys the idea of cross referencing without standing on another article to add validity to yours. Remember, the Foundation is all about secrecy. Researchers on one SCP might not even know the others that exist. As for the strike-outs, these files are on computers. I recommend using collapsibles entitled something like &quot;Former Containment Procedures.&quot;</p>
<p>At first I was prepared to greatly dislike the concept. However, all of it, raps included, are fairly well written. And I enjoyed the story.</p>
<p>The cross-references at the end weaken your article. If I follow those links, not only am I going to compare your article against some that are going to be exemplary, I will forget the nuances of what you have to offer simply because I have been overloaded with information.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>I have to chew on this one a bit. I am hesitantly leaning toward the positive.</p>

							</div>

								
					
